  "articleBody": "Installing custom interior trim transforms a room from basic drywall into a polished, finished space. Whether you want bold baseboards, crown molding, or decorative casings, success comes down to careful planning before anyone cuts a board.\n\nKey Benefits\nCustom trim delivers tailored proportions that standard builder-grade trim simply lacks. It conceals structural transitions, protects high-traffic wall corners, and gives rooms architectural balance.\n\nProcess Breakdown\nA proper installation starts with evaluating existing wall plumb and checking moisture levels in the room. Materials must acclimate to avoid shrinkage after fastening. Next, the installer measures, dry-fits pieces, cuts precise miter joints, fastens the molding securely, fills fastener holes, and applies matching caulk before final priming and painting.\n\nWhat Can Change the Right Choice\nRoom humidity and room traffic dictate materials. Solid wood offers traditional grain and sharp profiles, but in humid bathrooms or basements, moisture-resistant alternatives prevent warping. Similarly, high-traffic hallways require durable stock that resists dents.\n\nReal Examples and Next Steps\nTying intricate casing around uneven drywall openings or matching existing heritage profiles in older homes requires a steady hand. Abraham Becerra and the crew at HH Remodeling LLC handle custom carpentry and interior painting across Aurora and the Denver area. Call 303-505-0866 to discuss your trim ideas.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
